# FIFA Extraplanetary Football Blueprint — Final Edition
### The Dribble Preservation Standard (DPS) · Complete System Architecture, Laws & Verification Protocol

---

## Preamble

This document is the final synthesis of the Dribble Preservation Standard framework, integrating original theoretical development, deep-field technology research across twelve domains, and the full hardware specification developed through iterative critical analysis. Every component, law, and parameter traces directly back to a single non-negotiable objective: to preserve the sensory, temporal, and biomechanical identity of football as it exists on Earth, in a gravity environment that is 38% (Mars) or 16% (Moon) of the terrestrial baseline.

This is not an adaptation of football. It is an engineering defence of football against physics.

---

## Part I — Design Philosophy and the Four Invariants

The foundational insight of this project is that dribbling is not a visual aesthetic. It is a physical system with four coupled properties that must hold simultaneously. If any one collapses, dribbling ceases to exist as a recognisable skill.

**DPS Invariant I — Touch-Timing:** The interval between successive ball-foot contacts during dribbling must remain within 5 ms RMS of Earth reference (120–200 ms cadence). Low gravity extends ball flight time by a factor of 1.62× on Mars and 2.46× on the Moon for identical touch force. Without correction, the ball disappears from control range and the dribbling rhythm disintegrates.

**DPS Invariant II — Lateral Cut Force:** The impulse available to change direction during a plant-foot cut is proportional to the normal force on the foot, which is proportional to gravity. At Martian gravity, unaugmented lateral cut force drops to 38% of Earth baseline. A player cannot execute a body feint, an elastico, or a directional change with less than 40% of the force the move was designed for. This requires active pitch-boot coupling to restore.

**DPS Invariant III — Plant-Foot Stability:** The plant foot must not slip more than 2 cm during a cut. At Mars gravity, the maximum static friction force on an unaugmented boot drops to approximately 182 N (from 480 N on Earth for a 70 kg player with μ = 0.7). Defenders cannot defend. Attackers cannot push off. The sport degrades into a slow-motion floating contest.

**DPS Invariant IV — Ball-Return Predictability:** The ball must return to the foot at a predictable height and time for every touch class. A coefficient of restitution tuned to Earth conditions produces chaotic bounce heights in low gravity. The ball return must be engineered to match the temporal and spatial expectation the player's motor system has built over years of practice.

These four invariants are the engineering specification. The system is certified only when all four hold simultaneously in full-match simulation.

