## Part III — Layer 0: DPS-0 Matchball

The DPS-0 matchball is a mechatronic system. It is not a passive object. Its two primary functions are restitution control and Magnus force emulation.

**Electrorheological Bladder.** The ball's inner chamber contains a low-density silicone-oil base fluid with a polarisable nanoparticle suspension. Carbon-nanotube flexible electrode layers line the inner bladder wall. Applied voltage (0–5 kV mm⁻¹) alters fluid viscosity across four orders of magnitude: from approximately 10 mPa·s in the unpowered state to approximately 10⁴ Pa·s at saturation (5 kV mm⁻¹). Transition time is less than 3 ms.

The ball operates in two software-defined states:

*Dribble mode:* ER fluid in high-viscosity state (field ~3.5 kV mm⁻¹). Effective restitution coefficient e = 0.62 ± 0.02. The ball is deliberately soft on ground contact — kinetic energy is absorbed, bounce height is suppressed to Mars-cadence requirement, and the ball stays in the player's foot-contact zone. This mode is triggered by any contact of force < 150 N or duration < 12 ms.

*Strike/Pass mode:* ER field removed. Fluid returns to low-viscosity baseline. Effective restitution e = 0.88 ± 0.03. The ball is lively and full energy transfer is available for passes, shots, and clearances. This mode is triggered by contact force > 150 N sustained for more than 12 ms. Transition from dribble to strike mode completes in ≤ 2.5 ms after threshold detection.

This state duality solves what was called the Dead-Ball Paradox: a universally low-restitution ball would preserve dribbling but kill the passing and shooting game. The ER fluid switch preserves both.

**CMG Magnus Effect Emulator.** Mars atmospheric density renders aerodynamic spin-curve generation negligible. The solution is an internal tri-axial Control Moment Gyroscope suspended at the geometric centre of the ball on a carbon-fibre gimbal. A 9-axis IMU at 1 kHz tracks the ball's shell spin vector ω and free-flight trajectory in real time. The CMG precesses to exert a controlled internal torque on the ball body, producing a net lateral force vector F_sim = k·(ω × v)/m, where k ≈ 4.5 × 10⁻⁴ kg·m, calibrated to Earth-standard Magnus magnitude. Lateral force authority reaches 0.15 N at ball speeds of 30 m s⁻¹. Trajectory error remains below 5% relative to Earth-equivalent spin curves.

Angular momentum capacity: 4.2 N·m·s. Maximum rotor speed: 18,000 RPM. Fallback option: 24 tangential micro-valves (0.3 mm exit, 20 g CO₂ cartridge, 4 bar, piezoelectric actuation < 1 ms) is retained as an alternative for tournament rules prohibiting internal flywheels.

**Physical and power parameters.** Total mass: 430 g ± 2 g, matching Earth standard including all internal systems. Circumference: 685–695 mm. Power: 3.7 V, 500 mAh solid-state battery, inductively recharged via pitch mat. Active endurance: 120 minutes. Sensing: 9-axis IMU (1 kHz), 8-point contact pressure map, shell deformation strain gauges. Fault signalling: amber LED array and acoustic pulse for any CMG saturation, IMU latency spike > 2.5 ms, or ER field collapse.

The lineage of this system is traceable to the Adidas Connected Ball (TDK ICM-20649 IMU, 500 Hz sampling, deployed at World Cup 2022 and Euro 2024) and the Cairos-Adidas electromagnetic pitch-ball coupling already embedded in professional infrastructure. The DPS-0 extends and builds on hardware that is commercially proven.

