## Part V — Layer 2: D-Boot (Vector Boot)

The boot is a phase-aware wearable that switches between two distinct biomechanical roles within a single stride cycle: soft ball-feel during touch, Earth-equivalent ground anchor during plant.

**Outsole Architecture.** The outsole is divided into two functionally separate zones. The touch zone — medial forefoot and toe box — uses soft natural rubber with no magnetic elements, preserving the tactile sensitivity essential to close control. The plant zone — heel and lateral forefoot — uses a compliant ferromagnetic composite pad that interlocks with the MRE pitch field when the pitch coil is energised beneath it.

**Adhesion Stack.** The plant zone incorporates a hybrid adhesion system combining gecko-inspired dry fibrillar microstructures (van der Waals adhesion) with electroadhesive voltage augmentation. This architecture is directly derived from the geCKo Materials system, which has been tested on six space missions, holds 16 lbs per square inch, and achieves 120,000+ attach-detach cycles with zero residue. Electroadhesive augmentation — validated in peer-reviewed work at IOP Smart Materials (2024) — adds 72% greater grip force over passive gecko adhesion alone. The adhesion increases naturally in the direction of shear loading during lateral cuts (directional engagement, as in real gecko feet) and diminishes on lift-off without requiring active control. Electropermanent magnets in the plant zone heel, derived from the MARVEL quadrupedal climbing robot foot architecture (Science Robotics, 2023), provide supplementary holding force with zero power consumption in the held state.

**Midsole Sensing and Phase Classification.** A 16-cell force-sensitive resistor matrix captures normal and shear stress at 1 kHz. A 9-axis IMU sits in the heel counter. Onboard microcontroller classifies each ground contact within 3 ms using two criteria: heel load exceeding 40% of body weight combined with predicted contact duration exceeding 80 ms signals a plant event; all other contacts are classified as dribble touch or general locomotion. The classification is transmitted to pitch and vest via UWB link at less than 2 ms latency, 500 Hz update rate.

**MRE Studs.** Each stud contains magnetorheological elastomer with an embedded induction pickup coil. During touch phase, studs remain compliant. During plant phase, the pitch coil beneath energises the stud MRE, rigidifying it in lock-step with the surface field, maximising contact area and friction at the exact moment of maximum lateral force demand.

**Power.** A kinetic energy harvester in the midsole charges a supercapacitor during locomotion. No replaceable battery. Maintenance-free operation across a full match. Boot mass: less than 300 g per unit.

