## Part VI — Layer 3: Inertial Equilibrium Vest

Perfect foot-ground anchoring in reduced gravity creates a new failure mode not present on Earth: catastrophic upper-body over-rotation. When the feet grip hard and the legs push laterally with Earth-equivalent force, but gravity provides only 38% of the downward load on the torso, the body pivots violently around the fixed lower limb. This is the Torso Shear Crisis. The Inertial Equilibrium Vest solves it.

**Structure.** A carbon-fibre harness with integrated dorsal plate houses two orthogonal control moment gyroscopes on the yaw and roll axes, positioned close to the spine to minimise moment arm to the body's centre of mass. Total system mass: 1.8 kg, balanced around the player's centre of mass.

**Actuators.** Each flywheel: titanium, 400 g, 120 mm diameter. Maximum rotor speed: 15,000 RPM. Gimbal torque motor peak: 40 Nm per gyro. Combined counter-torque authority: 250 Nm in yaw, 150 Nm in roll. An IMU on the spinal mount measures torso angular velocity and runs a local feedback correction loop.

**Predictive Control.** The pitch prediction server calculates the required counter-torque from the player's biomechanical model — verified lean mass, centre-of-mass height, and intended cut direction from the neural network — and transmits the torque vector to the vest 10 ms before predicted plant strike. The vest executes the corrective torque within 5 ms of trigger, actively stabilising the upper body through the cut, then decays smoothly over 200 ms. This sequence is deterministic and runs at 100 Hz.

**Safety.** Redundant electromechanical brakes lock flywheels within 10 ms on power loss, fault detection, or activation of the referee's remote deadman switch. If flywheels reach maximum RPM (saturation), an audible-haptic alert warns the player to reduce cut intensity; the referee's HUD is simultaneously notified. In any failure mode, the vest becomes a passive 1.8 kg weight — neutral, not harmful.

