## Part VII — Layer 4: Infrastructure

**Pressurised Dome.** The dome is not merely habitat — it is the primary aerodynamic solution. A dome maintained at 1 atm Earth-standard internal atmosphere makes the ball's aerodynamic behaviour inside it identical to Earth: same drag coefficients, same Magnus lift magnitudes, same spin-curve curvatures. The CMG emulator in the ball then functions as backup rather than primary system. Dome architecture derived from BIG's Mars city studies uses hybrid concrete structural base with pressurised glass dome enclosure. At 0.38g (Mars) or 0.16g (Moon), structural loads on the dome are dramatically reduced, enabling larger span structures with the same material budget. A full-size pitch (105 m × 68 m) requires a dome span of approximately 130 m — technically achievable and structurally less demanding than equivalent Earth spans.

**Lava Tube Variant.** Lunar and Martian lava tubes — confirmed by JAXA SELENE radar surveys — are natural underground caverns with roofs up to 9 m thick. Sufficiently wide tubes can be pressurised with airlock and seal infrastructure, providing a radiation-shielded, thermally stable, pre-formed enclosure. The ideal long-term Mars football arena is a pressurised lava tube: the geological structure supplies the enclosure, and the engineers supply only atmosphere and playing systems.

**Earth Training Protocol.** Mars-deployed players must begin motor-pattern adaptation on Earth using AlterG Anti-Gravity Treadmill systems, which reduce effective body weight from 100% to 20% (corresponding to Moon gravity). Mars-specific conditioning runs at 38% body weight. VR headsets displaying a Mars pitch environment are used simultaneously: research (Frontiers Neuroscience, 2023) confirms that sensorimotor coherence between visual flow and gravity reduction is essential for accurate motor adaptation. Without the VR component, the brain adapts the locomotion but not the spatial perception. Estimated motor re-learning curve on Mars after Earth training: 30–90 days in-habitat.

