## Part VIII — System Integration and Communication

All active nodes — ball, boots, vest, pitch grid, and referee HUD — operate on a private 5G-URLLC (Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency Communications) network slice with end-to-end latency below 5 ms and 99.9999% reliability. Time synchronisation uses IEEE 802.1AS Precision Time Protocol with jitter below 1 μs across all nodes.

**Key data flows (all time-stamped to PTP clock):**

Boot → Pitch Grid: phase classification and local foot trajectory prediction (500 Hz).
Pitch Grid → Vest: plant trigger plus desired torque vector, issued 10 ms before predicted contact (100 Hz).
Ball → Pitch Grid: state vector, restitution mode, CMG status, fault flags (100 Hz).
Pitch Grid → Referee HUD: infraction alerts, cadence drift events, telemetry anomalies (real-time).

**Latency budget:**

- ER fluid state change: ≤ 2.5 ms from contact detection
- Pitch coil field rise: < 3 ms from energisation signal
- Boot phase classification: ≤ 3 ms from first contact
- UWB boot-to-pitch transmission: < 2 ms
- Vest torque onset: ≤ 5 ms from trigger receipt
- Ball fault detection to whistle: < 100 ms

Every timing margin in this budget has been derived from the physical constraints of the system: the coil inductance, the ER fluid response curve, the CMG precession dynamics, and the human perceptual threshold for touch-timing deviation (approximately 10 ms JND for trained players).

