## Part X — System Verification and Certification

A complete DPS-compliant installation — field, ball, and player equipment — is certified when the following six criteria hold simultaneously during a full-match simulation or standardised certification protocol:

**V1 — Touch-Timing Invariance:** Distribution of ball-foot touch intervals during dribbling sequences matches Earth reference (120–200 ms cadence envelope) with RMS deviation less than 5 ms.

**V2 — Lateral Cut Force Equivalence:** Peak plant-foot lateral force impulse during a standardised 45° direction change at 5 m s⁻¹ is within 10% of Earth-measured baseline for the same player at Earth gravity.

**V3 — Plant-Foot Slip Limit:** No slip displacement exceeding 2 cm occurs during cuts, excluding deliberate Traction Locking infractions which are handled under Law 12.

**V4 — Torso Equilibrium:** No overturning event occurs where the IEV's recovery window exceeds 200 ms duration, or where residual torso angular velocity exceeds 0.5 rad s⁻¹ measured at 200 ms post-cut.

**V5 — Ball Trajectory Fidelity:** For any spin vector and speed combination within match-realistic parameters, in-flight curvature error relative to Earth-equivalent Magnus trajectory is 5% or less.

**V6 — System Latency Budget:** All active components meet their specified latency margins: ball state change ≤ 2.5 ms; pitch field rise < 3 ms; vest torque onset ≤ 5 ms post-trigger; boot phase classification ≤ 3 ms.

The DPS is met when the game feels fast, close, rhythmically alive, and continuously dangerous — when a dribbler can beat a defender in a phone-booth-sized space at 0.38g the same way they can at 1g, and when a perfectly struck curling free kick bends into the top corner just as it would above a green Earth pitch under a blue sky. The physics of the planet have changed entirely. The game has not.

